# Testing

## Objective

Write tests that actually catch regressions, matching the project's existing test framework and conventions.

## Core Principles

- Match the existing test framework/runner — don't introduce a second one.
- Test behavior and edge cases, not implementation details.
- New logic branches (especially error paths) get a corresponding test.
- Tests must be deterministic — no reliance on real time, network, or execution order.

## Requirements

- Any new bug fix includes a regression test that fails without the fix.
- New public functions/endpoints get at least one happy-path and one failure-path test.
- Mocks/stubs are used for external dependencies (network, DB, filesystem) unless the test is explicitly an integration test.
- Test names describe the scenario and expected outcome, not just the function name.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Writing tests that only assert 'it doesn't throw' without checking actual output.
- Testing private implementation details that make refactors break tests unnecessarily.
- Skipping tests for error/edge-case paths.

## Self Review

Before considering work complete, verify:

✓ New/changed logic has corresponding tests, including at least one edge case.
✓ Tests pass deterministically, not flakily.
✓ Test style/framework matches the rest of the suite.

## Golden Rule

A test suite is only valuable if it fails when the code is actually broken.
